WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.890 --> 00:00:04.770
Welcome to PyMOL 101 with Molecular Memory!

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:10.730
In this lesson, we’ll explore the less active
conformation of phosphofructokinase bound

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:12.120
to an inhibitor.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:18.260
We’ll use the inhibitor to generate a binding
site, instead of selecting residues given

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:22.360
to us in a manuscript, like we did in Lesson
2.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:26.570
This is helpful when we may not have access
to an article.

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:31.230
Load the biological assembly of 6pfk in PyMOL.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:38.290
File, get PDB, 6pfk, assembly 1.

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:45.000
Then, color by chain with CPK coloring and
display the sequence.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.949
Scroll until you see the three letter codes
for the inhibitor molecules.

00:00:50.949 --> 00:00:57.079
Notice the PGA ligands have taken on the color
of a chain that they’re close to.

00:00:57.079 --> 00:01:01.330
We’ll model the interactions of just one
of the ligands.

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:06.870
Let’s select the cyan PGA, this light blue
green one here.

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:14.650
The letters sele pop up—in Lesson 2, we
learned to rename selections to save them.

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:16.549
A, rename.

00:01:16.549 --> 00:01:22.689
Delete the letters sele that come up in the
upper left of the structure viewer window.

00:01:22.689 --> 00:01:26.120
Type inhibitor and press enter.

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:31.150
Now I’ll click in the blank space to make
sure I don’t add anything unwanted to this

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:32.560
selection.

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:36.970
Let’s zoom in on the inhibitor: A, zoom.

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:42.869
I’ll use the pinch motion on my trackpad
to zoom out a bit—if you’re using a mouse,

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:44.040
right click will zoom.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:48.600
There’s a too much clipping to really see
the binding site, so I’ll adjust that by

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:51.350
running two fingers up and down the trackpad.

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:58.360
Now, we can see the inhibitor is actually
binding between two chains of the protein.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:02.680
If we want to focus on just the parts of the
structure the ligand is interacting with,

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:05.600
we can hide the other chains.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:10.970
This isn’t 100% necessary here, but I really
want to show you some of the options in the

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:12.640
mouse control menu.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:16.700
Click where it says Selecting Residues, and
the menu changes.

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:19.280
Let’s stop on chains.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:23.270
Click on the inhibitor and two chains in the
structure viewer.

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:27.940
You can also select by clicking in the sequence
display menu.

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:32.750
Zoom out a bit, and then next to SELE, hide
, unselected.

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:38.769
Now, we’re going to copy the inhibitor selection
and modify it.

00:02:38.769 --> 00:02:40.480
Inhibitor, A, duplicate.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:46.720
A new selection, sel01, pops up.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:51.830
We’ll turn this into the binding site, so
let’s rename it “binding”.

00:02:51.830 --> 00:03:01.120
To select nearby residues: A, modify, around,
residues within 4 angstroms to capture all

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:04.340
hydrogen bonding amino acids.

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:09.489
If you choose a 5 angstrom sphere, that will
capture residues that may be interaction through

00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:12.180
van der Waals interactions too.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:17.959
Now, show sticks and zoom in on the binding
site.

00:03:17.959 --> 00:03:24.060
To select binding site water molecules only,
we can modify the binding site selection we

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:26.270
just made.

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:30.150
Duplicate the selection called binding.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:34.349
Rename sel02 to active water, A, rename, delete
SELE, type active water.

00:03:34.349 --> 00:03:42.220
Now: Action button, modify, restrict, to solvent.

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:45.000
And let’s show these as spheres.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:46.890
Time for some adjustments.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:50.709
The water spheres are large and obscuring
the active site.

00:03:50.709 --> 00:03:52.780
Resize them using a preset.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:56.000
A, preset, ball and stick.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:00.610
The inhibitor’s cyan carbons are blending
in with some of the binding site residues.

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:08.250
Let’s recolor them: C, by element, and I’ll
select this one with white carbons.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:11.750
Now, to add some interactions!

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:17.730
Inhibitor, A button, find, polar contacts.

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:23.050
There are a bunch of choices here, but let’s
just choose “to any atoms”.

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:28.430
And we can indeed see interactions with both
protein chains!

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:33.560
If we’d like, we can change the line color,
let’s try magenta.

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:39.650
And as we’re getting closer to a publication-quality
image, let’s go ahead and change the background

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:41.229
to white.

00:04:41.229 --> 00:04:46.020
Display dropdown, background, white.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:52.449
If we want to remove the nearby residues that
aren’t making polar contacts, we can, even

00:04:52.449 --> 00:04:55.680
though they’re close enough to have van
der Waals interactions with the ligand.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:01.639
First, we need to change our mouse control
menu back to the default “residues”.

00:05:01.639 --> 00:05:05.640
Click here in the mouse control menu until
it reads “residues”.

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:09.050
Now, click on “binding” in the names panel.

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:14.360
Carefully deselect any residues that don’t
have dotted lines going to them by clicking

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:15.360
on them.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:18.170
You’ll need to rotate and zoom while doing
this.

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:22.750
And if you accidentally deselect something
you didn’t mean to, just click on it again

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:24.230
to add it back.

00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:29.710
OK, I think everything still selected has
polar interactions now.

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:35.190
The best way to show our modified binding
site is to hide all the sticks, then add back

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:37.819
the inhibitor and modified active site.

00:05:37.819 --> 00:05:41.560
First, 6pfk, hide, sticks.

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:46.289
Now, let’s add the hydrogen bonding residues
back.

00:05:46.289 --> 00:05:48.810
Inhibitor, show, sticks.

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:51.330
Binding, show, sticks.

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:55.539
Finally, click in the blank space to deselect
everything.

00:05:55.539 --> 00:06:00.949
Notice this arginine is interacting through
its side chain, but we’re showing the entire

00:06:00.949 --> 00:06:04.370
residue with its backbone atoms.

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:09.139
We can clean this up a bit more by just showing
the side chain.

00:06:09.139 --> 00:06:14.550
And we can do this one or two residues at
a time, without saving the selection.

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:22.010
Click on the appropriate residues, sele, hide,
main chain.

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:23.010
Looking good!

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:25.780
We can delete this selection if we want now.

00:06:25.780 --> 00:06:30.080
We want someone looking at our image to be
able to easily interpret it.

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:34.710
Let’s label the amino acid residues of the
binding site.

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:37.440
Binding, L, residues.

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:41.490
Ok, there they are, but some are super hard
to see.

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:44.330
We’re going to move them using edit mode.

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:50.080
To start, I remove all clipping, and find
a relatively unobscured view of the binding

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:54.080
site where all of the residues can be seen
clearly.

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:59.680
Now, zoom the binding site to let PyMOL add
its own clipping back.

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:03.810
Now, YOU NEED TO SAVE YOUR SESSION.

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:06.410
This is an essential save point.

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:11.080
You can completely mess up the protein in
edit mode, and you can’t undo it.

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:15.990
So, take a moment to save your PyMOL .PSE
session file.

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:17.370
Control save.

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:22.870
In the mouse controls, the mouse mode can
be changed from viewing to an editing mode

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:23.930
by clicking.

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:31.220
Now, if I press command on my Mac keyboard,
and click and drag on the labels, I can reposition

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:32.220
them.

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:38.470
But, if you accidentally click the protein,
you can move it, completely messing up the

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:39.470
structure!

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:45.710
So, I encourage you to save after each and
every label move.

00:07:45.710 --> 00:07:46.710
Awesome job.

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:48.090
That’s a ton of work!

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:52.330
First, click on the mouse controls to exit
edit mode.

00:07:52.330 --> 00:07:55.540
Now, save a scene of your hard work.

00:07:55.540 --> 00:08:01.030
In the dropdown menus: Scene, store, F1.

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:04.100
Right click on the scene and rename it.

00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:09.550
It’s really tricky to show an active site
without a protein loop getting in the way

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:11.889
or some poor looking clipping.

00:08:11.889 --> 00:08:17.230
To be honest, I sometimes completely hide
the amino acid residues in a loop that’s

00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:18.330
getting in the way.

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:23.810
I really like the context the cartoon gives,
but another strategy is to hide the cartoon

00:08:23.810 --> 00:08:24.930
completely.

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:28.289
6pfk, H, cartoon.

00:08:28.289 --> 00:08:34.870
Now, we can remove clipping completely, and
the active site resides just seem to float

00:08:34.870 --> 00:08:35.870
there.

00:08:35.870 --> 00:08:37.649
Let’s save a scene.

00:08:37.649 --> 00:08:44.230
Now, you can toggle back and forth and choose
which scene you prefer, or try to modify further.

00:08:44.230 --> 00:08:48.990
Thanks for watching this episode of PyMOL
101.

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:51.600
Next time, we’ll learn about the measurement
wizard.

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:54.660
If you’re enjoying this series, please subscribe
to my channel.

